{
  "_note": "Sign-based inn/tavern name vocabulary. Names are built from a charge (the painted device on the sign) plus optional color, number, posture, pairing, body-part, haunt, or guild arms, following the structural syntax of medieval and Renaissance English tavern signs. Consumed by src/innname.js. See docs/DESIGN.md 'Inn names' for the model, tuning, and how to add charges. Every charge lists concrete biomes (this project's five) and the tiers it can appear at; keep each biome stocked with charges that reach 'noble' so high-tier pools stay varied and biome-flavored rather than collapsing onto a handful of universal devices. A 'sign' is a bare, color-neutral noun phrase (no leading article): innname.js adds the article and injects the chosen color, so do not bake a tincture into a charge that has color:true. A pattern with a 'tiers' list only fires at those tiers. 'haunts' on a charge or trade feeds the possessive pattern (The Fox's Den); 'arms_sign' on a trade feeds the arms pattern (The Miller's Arms); 'waypoints' plus 'ordinals' feed the waypoint pattern (The Last Shade). Any charge, figure, or trade may carry a 'weight' (default 1); use values below 1 to damp devices that sit in several biome pools at once.",

  "tuning": {
    "designator_chance": 0.6,
    "archaic_color_chance": 0.15,
    "posture_chance": 0.5,
    "hoop_suffix_chance": 0.01,
    "genitive_number_chance": 0.4
  },

  "patterns": [
    { "id": "single",     "weight": 56 },
    { "id": "color",      "weight": 16 },
    { "id": "number",     "weight": 8 },
    { "id": "possessive", "weight": 8, "tiers": ["roadside", "common"] },
    { "id": "waypoint",   "weight": 5, "tiers": ["roadside", "common"] },
    { "id": "arms",       "weight": 6, "tiers": ["common", "fine", "noble"] },
    { "id": "pair",       "weight": 5 },
    { "id": "on_object",  "weight": 3 },
    { "id": "body_part",  "weight": 2 },
    { "id": "genitive",   "weight": 0, "tiers": ["common", "fine", "noble"] }
  ],

  "_pattern_biome_weights_note": "Per-biome overrides of the global pattern weights above; an unlisted pattern keeps its global weight, and a resolved weight of 0 removes the pattern for that biome. Arid inverts the English-signboard defaults: it leads with the genitive 'The Khan of the Two Palms' grammar, leans on waypoint and possessive (caravan-route and patron naming), keeps only a little of the heraldic color/single register, and drops on_object entirely.",
  "pattern_biome_weights": {
    "arid": {
      "genitive":   30,
      "possessive": 20,
      "waypoint":   15,
      "single":     20,
      "number":      8,
      "arms":        5,
      "color":       2,
      "on_object":   0,
      "body_part":   2
    }
  },

